//! LiberSystem observability package - system graph and supervisor services.

package liber:observability@1;

use liber:base@1.{error};

/// The class of a System Graph node: a userspace system service, a userspace device
/// driver, or a hardware device node. The label that turns the boot dependency tree
/// into a typed, classified graph.
enum component-type {
	service,
	driver,
	device,
}

/// A component's liveness, derived live from the kernel - a running process, a clean
/// exit (stopped), or a fault / kill (failed) - plus the supervisor's transient view
/// (restarting between attempts, pending before the first start). A crash or stop
/// surfaces here at the next snapshot, without the component self-reporting it.
enum component-state {
	running,
	stopped,
	failed,
	restarting,
	pending,
}

/// The per-component counters the kernel tracks for a process: its IPC volume (channel
/// messages sent and received), how many handles its table holds, how many bytes of
/// user memory it has mapped, plus the supervisor's history of it - how many times it
/// has been restarted, how many times the watchdog found it hung and acted, and the
/// kind of its last failure ("" if it has never failed). The kernel-derived columns are
/// live; the supervisor columns are merged in from the `supervisor` interface. The typed
/// form of the resource-usage and supervisor columns the `graph` command renders.
record counters {
	messages-sent:     u64,
	messages-received: u64,
	handles:           u64,
	memory-bytes:      u64,
	restarts:          u32,
	watchdog-trips:    u32,
	last-failure:      string,
}

/// One bounded count a component reports about ITSELF, which the kernel charges nobody for.
///
/// THE KERNEL'S COUNTERS ARE THE ONES ABOVE and they cover what it charges: messages, handles,
/// mapped bytes. A service also holds structures the kernel knows nothing about - a display
/// service's surfaces and present queues, a session service's sessions - and those are exactly what
/// an adversarial client multiplies. A count on its own says nothing, so each carries the BOUND it
/// is held to: `live` at `bound` is a component refusing new work, which is a different fact from
/// `live` being large.
///
/// OBSERVATION AND NEVER ENFORCEMENT. The bound is enforced where the structure is allocated; this
/// is the number a reader sees, and reading it changes nothing.
record resource-count {
	/// What is being counted, as the component names it (`surfaces`, `present-images`, ...).
	name:  string,
	live:  u64,
	bound: u64,
}

/// One node in the System Graph: a component's name, its type, its live state, the
/// names of the components it depends on (the graph edges), and its counters. A
/// service or driver carries live kernel counters; a device node carries the device's
/// identity with zero counters.
record component {
	name:      string,
	type:      component-type,
	state:     component-state,
	deps:      list<string>,
	counters:  counters,
	/// What this component reports about structures the kernel charges nobody for. EMPTY for a
	/// component that reports none, which is every one that has nothing an adversary can multiply.
	resources: list<resource-count>,
}

/// One lightweight trace span recorded while building a snapshot: the downstream call
/// it measured (e.g. "device.list", "process.stats") and how long it took in
/// nanoseconds. The spans make the cost of a snapshot's service calls queryable over
/// the same typed API.
record trace-span {
	name:        string,
	duration-ns: u64,
}

/// One device interrupt identity that woke a core, and how many times it did: the IDT vector on
/// x86_64 (a legacy IRQ n is 0x20 + n), the INTID on aarch64, the interrupt-file identity on
/// riscv64.
record core-wake-source {
	source: u32,
	count:  u64,
}

/// HOW ONE CORE RESTS, from the kernel's own record: the time it spent halted, how many times it
/// halted, and each wake under what ended it - its timer, an IPI, the housekeeping bound, or a
/// device's interrupt, in total and per identity. Wakes per second are two snapshots and the time
/// between them.
record core-idle {
	cpu:                u32,
	idle-ns:            u64,
	halts:              u64,
	wakes-timer:        u64,
	wakes-ipi:          u64,
	wakes-housekeeping: u64,
	wakes-device:       u64,
	@bound(8) sources:  list<core-wake-source>,
}

/// The whole live System Graph: every component node (services, drivers, devices) with
/// its edges, state and counters, plus the trace spans the snapshot recorded and each
/// core's idle record. The one typed value an edge node's observability serves, rendered
/// as CLI / JSON / CBOR.
record graph {
	components: list<component>,
	spans:      list<trace-span>,
	@bound(64) cores: list<core-idle>,
}

/// The SystemGraphService contract: serve the full live observability graph - every
/// component's state, dependencies and counters, plus the snapshot's trace spans - as
/// one typed value. The network-exposed (authenticated) remote-admin surface over the
/// same JSON / CBOR is a later phase; this is the local edge-node observability.
interface system-graph {
	@op(1) snapshot: func() -> result<graph, error>;
}

/// One component's supervisor state, as the ServiceManager supervisor tracks it: how
/// many times it has been restarted, how many times the watchdog found it hung (alive
/// but unresponsive) and acted, and the kind of its last failure ("" if it has never
/// failed). This is the supervisor's own view - restart counts and failure history a
/// component cannot self-report (a crashed component reports nothing) - which the
/// System Graph folds into each node's counters and the `graph` command renders.
record supervisor-stat {
	name:           string,
	/// What the supervisor OBSERVES: absent, starting, ready, stopping, stopped or failed. Six
	/// words, and `starting` and `stopping` are the two that had no name - a process that had been
	/// launched and had not reported in used to be called running, which is the one thing it
	/// certainly was not.
	state:          string,
	/// What the supervisor WANTS: running or stopped. A separate field, because a service somebody
	/// stopped on purpose and one that never came up are otherwise the same observation.
	desired:        string,
	/// This instance's epoch - the process koid, which the kernel hands out from a counter it
	/// never reuses. A restarted service has a different one, so an endpoint or a report from
	/// before the restart can be recognised as belonging to an instance that has ended.
	epoch:          u64,
	/// Why the last transition happened. A restart count says how often; this says what for.
	last-reason:    string,
	restarts:       u32,
	watchdog-trips: u32,
	last-failure:   string,
}

/// The ServiceManager supervisor contract: report the live supervisor state of every
/// managed component - restart counts, watchdog trips and last failure - over the typed
/// API. SystemGraphService queries this and merges it into the live graph, so the
/// observability surface carries the supervisor's history alongside the kernel's live
/// counters. The network-exposed remote-admin surface over the same data is a later phase.
interface supervisor {
	@op(1) status: func() -> result<list<supervisor-stat>, error>;
}

/// THE ONE LIVENESS EXCHANGE THE HARDWARE WATCHDOG IS FED BY.
///
/// ServiceManager serves it from its STANDING supervise loop's wait set, on a channel it hands the watchdog
/// service at each of that service's starts. An answer proves that the kernel schedules and delivers messages,
/// that ServiceManager's standing loop is not deadlocked, and - because the watchdog service asked - that its own
/// loop runs; the watchdog service pets its hardware only after an answer within its deadline. The loop answers
/// nothing while it runs a sequence - an orderly shutdown, a sleep transaction - and each of those carries a
/// step of its own for the watchdog.
interface supervisor-liveness {
	/// Answer `sequence`, which proves this answer is to this question.
	@op(1) alive: func(sequence: u64) -> result<u64, error>;
}
